  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and comfortable humidity\nWednesday. Low chance for a few showers for the south coast \nand Cape and Islands Wednesday. Otherwise, dry.\n\nMost areas are likely to remain dry for the remainder of the\nafternoon and evening. The one exception is the chance for a\nstray shower or storm to make it into northwest CT late\nafternoon/early evening. Overnight temperatures fall into the\n60s with periods of cloud cover. \n\nThere is still lower confidence in the potential for an offshore\nlow pressure system to brush across the southern portion of\nsouthern New England on Wednesday. This has been reflected\namongst deterministic and ensemble guidance with some solutions \ndeveloping a low pressure that tracks north to bring showers to \nthe south coast and Cape/Islands. Other solutions don't develop \na \"stronger\" surface low at all or track it too far south to \nbring shower chances to southern New England. GEFS probabilities\nfor > .01\" range 30 to 60 percent with probabilities dropping \noff quickly as you move north of the immediate coast. ECMWF \nprobabilities are > 20%. The NAM and RRFS have trended the most \nnorth bringing a more robust batch of shores across at least \nsouth and southeast southern New England. Given the spread here,\nworst case will be a some light showers Wednesday AM/early \nafternoon with the greater risk for the south coast and \nCape/Islands. If this system passes mainly south, expect a drier\nday. With flow aloft becoming more zonal, temperatures cool \nslightly bringing more seasonable highs Wednesday in the low to \nmid 80s. \n\nThere will likely be a shift to a more unsettled weather pattern  \nlate Wednesday into Thursday with indications of a frontal\nboundary stalling across a portion of southern New England. A\nfew embedded weak disturbances ride this boundary, resulting in \nperiods of showers arriving as early as overnight Wednesday \nnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances increase later Thursday into Friday\nas a front lingers near the south coast of New England. High\npressure then returns drier and cooler weather for this weekend. \nRain chances may increase again early next week as a low\npressure passes through.\n\nStill some timing differences to be worked out Thursday into \nFriday. Most of the latest global guidance suite has a cold \nfront near the south coast of New England Thursday morning. The \ndifferences arise from how quickly this front gets south enough \nof our region to no longer be a factor in our weather. The \nconsensus approach keeps this front close to the south coast\ninto Friday morning, but this timing could be quicker, leading\nto a drier day Friday. Not a lot of humidity to work with, but\nthinking there is at least a risk for diurnal showers and \nthunderstorms. A good portion of this time should remain dry for\nmost.\n\nThis front should finally push offshore later Friday, allowing \nhigh pressure to build across southern New England. This leads \nto drier and cooler conditions this weekend. The timing for \nearly next week is not certain as well. While there are some \ndifferences in the timing, the overall consensus is for a low \npressure to move across northern New England some time Monday. \nThis system should be off to the east by Tuesday. This timing \ncould change with later forecasts.",
